 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 49,
   "y": 8,
   "text": "CARRICK-A-REDE. Fishermen have strung a rope bridge to this island for over 250 years. Today's is 20 metres long and 30 metres above the sea. Don't look down. Look at the gulls. They look down for you."
  },
  {
   "x": 29,
   "y": 40,
   "text": "THE CAUSEWAY COASTAL ROUTE. Nine glens, a rope bridge, a lane of beech trees, and some of the best views in the islands. Take your time. The sea is not going anywhere."
  },
  {
   "x": 35,
   "y": 28,
   "text": "THE DARK HEDGES. Beech trees planted in the 1700s by the Stuart family to impress their visitors. Two hundred years later they impress everyone else. The branches meet overhead like a tunnel, and the road is rather moody."
  },
  {
   "x": 17,
   "y": 29,
   "text": "GLENARIFF. One of the nine Glens of Antrim, nicknamed the Queen of the Glens. There are waterfalls, ferns and paths that were built by walkers who would have preferred a shorter route."
  },
  {
   "x": 60,
   "y": 8,
   "text": "ISLAND LOOKOUT. The cliffs here are chalk topped with basalt, and the birds know it. Fulmars, kittiwakes and a very unimpressed raven."
  }
 ],
